<h2>The recovery and the anomaly</h2>
<p>The prosecution alleged recovery of 14.50 grams of smack — an intermediate quantity under the NDPS Act — leading to the applicant&rsquo;s arrest on 1 August 2026 and registration of Case Crime No. 48 of 2026 at PS Buggawala, Haridwar. The defence pointed to the case papers themselves: the inventory report and the arrest memo, which on the prosecution&rsquo;s own sequence were prepared before the FIR came to be registered, already bore the FIR number. The Court found in this a prima facie procedural irregularity going to the credibility of the documentation of recovery.</p>

<h2>The legal frame</h2>
<p>Three strands structured the order. First, quantity: below commercial quantity, the stringent twin conditions of Section 37 NDPS Act do not control, and bail is assessed on general principles — nature of the allegation, period of custody, antecedents and the risk calculus. Second, the irregularity: relying on <strong>Narcotics Control Bureau v. Kashif</strong> (2024), the Court held the documentary anachronism relevant to bail notwithstanding Section 37, since the Supreme Court has required courts to take serious note of investigative lapses in NDPS matters. Third, judicial restraint: the order is explicit that no view on the merits was being expressed — the irregularity earned release from custody, not an acquittal.</p>

<h2>Why paperwork sequence matters in NDPS cases</h2>
<p>NDPS prosecutions are built on a documented chain: information, search authorisation, recovery, seizure, inventory, sampling, FIR and arrest. Because conviction often rests on the recovery alone, courts scrutinise whether the paper trail could have been generated in the order claimed. A seizure memo that quotes an FIR number before the FIR exists, samples drawn without the mandated procedure, or inventories certified out of sequence each corrode the foundation. At the bail stage, such defects need only raise reasonable doubt about the prosecution&rsquo;s narrative; at trial, their weight is tested through the investigating officers&rsquo; cross-examination.</p>
<table class="law">
